@@ -17,3 +17,5 @@
 - (2026-10-03, #22) The checker requires block-style empty lists (`prs:` with nothing after), not `[]`, and rejects `current: yes` without a non-`none` destination, so a historical-only entry must use `current: no`. (Empty lists are block style (`prs:` with nothing after); a historical-only knowledge entry uses `current: no` (the validator rejects `current: yes` without a real destination).)
 - (2026-10-03, #24) Compute every duration from the gh timestamps and write both timestamps in the record: the record said "eleven minutes" but created_at 2025-12-24T11:32:02Z and closed_at 11:52:27Z are 20 minutes 25 seconds apart (third audit in a row with a wrong duration). (Compute every duration from gh createdAt/closedAt and write both timestamps; never state a duration from memory (wrong in audits #22, #23 and #24).)
 - (2026-10-03, #24) A pre-draft lists packages straight from an unticked template checklist (here `Encina.Caching.*`, `Encina.AspNetCore` etc. were all unchecked); take packages from the code that shipped, not from the template. (Take the record's packages from the code that shipped, never from an unticked template checklist in the issue or the pre-draft.)
+- (2026-10-03, #25) The record validator requires `audit`, block-list (not `[]`) fields, and a non-`none` destination for `current: yes`; a duplicate record can use `docs` -> CHANGELOG.md as the destination. (Before writing a record, check it against knowledge-records.cs rules: `audit` present, block-list fields (never `[]`), and a non-`none` destination when `current: yes` (a duplicate can point `docs` at CHANGELOG.md).)
+- (2026-10-03, #26) An issue closed "created in error" is a duplicate of its same-scope re-creation (#42 here, #52 for audit #25): compare bodies, then record `outcome: duplicate` with `duplicate_of`, rather than `rejected-unexplained`. I first chose rejected-unexplained; state_reason COMPLETED and a different title ("enhancements" vs "enhanced DLQ handling") hid the match. (An issue closed "created in error" is a duplicate of its same-scope re-creation even when the title differs: search same-scope issues created soon after, compare bodies, and record `outcome: duplicate` with `duplicate_of` (audits #25 -> #52, #26 -> #42); never settle on rejected-unexplained before that search.)
diff --git a/.claude/agents/lessons/test-auditor.md b/.claude/agents/lessons/test-auditor.md
index e65fafca..6fb6e781 100644
--- a/.claude/agents/lessons/test-auditor.md
+++ b/.claude/agents/lessons/test-auditor.md
@@ -26,3 +26,8 @@
 - (2026-10-03, #22) A `## Findings` section with no findings contains only `- none`. Any prose after it, even an explanation that the gaps are informational, is parsed by the remediation drafter as a finding. Put informational gaps in a separate section such as `## Informational (not findings)`. (A Findings section with no findings contains only "- none"; informational gaps go in a separate "## Informational (not findings)" section (audit #22 FAIL).)
 - (2026-10-03, #23) A filter `FullyQualifiedName~EncinaFixtureTests` does not match `EncinaTestFixtureTests`; a class-name filter must be checked against the class list, and the Base\ duplicate folder makes every such filter run both copies. (Check a class-name test filter against the actual class list before running it; tests/Encina.UnitTests/Testing/Base duplicates Testing/ (#1693), so filters there run both copies.)
 - (2026-10-03, #24) For a closed-in-error issue with an empty diff, the archivist's "tests not located by file name" note is the one useful input: a `Select-String -List` over `tests\` for the successor's class names answers it in one call, and a narrow filtered run of the successor's namespaces confirms the tests pass; per-file gaps go to the successor's audit (#35), not to the empty issue. (Locate a successor's tests with one Select-String -List over tests\ for its class names before the filtered run.)
+- (2026-10-03, #25) For a closed-in-error issue whose duplicate delivered the code, the one-call `Select-String -List` over `tests\` for the duplicate's class names (lesson of #24) located 13 test files at once; a narrow filtered run of all four flags under Release was quick enough (the integration run itself reported 916 ms with Docker available), so run integration whenever Docker is up. (When Docker is up, run the integration flag too: a filtered integration run of a small feature takes about a second once built, so skipping it is never justified by time.)
+- (2026-10-03, #25) The filtered contract run needs the bare namespace (`Encina.ContractTests.Marten`) while the guard tests of the same feature live in two namespaces (`...Marten.Snapshots` and `...Infrastructure.Marten`); a name-based filter (`Snapshot&Marten`) caught both, a namespace-suffix filter would have dropped one. (Filter test runs by feature name (`FullyQualifiedName~Snapshot&FullyQualifiedName~Marten`) rather than by a namespace suffix: one feature's tests can live in several namespaces per flag.)
+- (2026-10-03, #25) stage tests: '- none' followed by prose; prose ignored (Under `## Findings` write the `- none` marker alone; any explanation goes under `## Informational (not findings)` (prose after the marker is ignored and recorded as a pipeline lesson since #1694).)
+- (2026-10-03, #25) A "the only references are" claim should come from a Grep over `tests\` for the symbol, not from the reflection tests alone. Mock set-ups also reference the method without executing the implementation, so say "no test executes it" and cite the measured zero lines. (Never claim "the only references are" from memory or from one test kind: Grep `tests\` for the symbol, and when the point is that nothing executes it, say "no test executes it" and cite the measured zero lines (mock set-ups reference a method without running it).)
+- (2026-10-03, #26) A `FullyQualifiedName~DeadLetter` filter matches the CDC dead-letter contract and property tests too (`Cdc\ICdcDeadLetterStoreContractTests.cs`, `Cdc\InMemoryCdcDeadLetterStorePropertyTests.cs`): a green contract or property count from a name filter does not mean the feature has contract or property tests. State which test files the passing tests belong to, and read the per-file lines executed. (A green count from a name filter is not evidence the feature has tests of that flag: list the test files the passing tests belong to and read the per-file lines executed (a `~DeadLetter` filter also matched the CDC dead-letter tests).)
